From d0e9ddbe7925196a3e532aaaa87f569f0a67b488 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:41:04 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] startup trace: name each step, the GPU chosen, and each GPU
 asked
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The idle scenario's own trace from the failing gate run (21de834) had
two lines: the waker firing at 600 ms, and the 20 s deadline. No window
event, no sleep decision, no frame — the event loop never reached its
first event, so the main thread was blocked in startup while the waker
thread ran on time. That rules out the occlusion theory (an occluded
window still logs events) and points at window or GPU bring-up, which
was not traced.

- app.rs `surface()`: `startup: creating window`, `window created
  (WxH); opening the GPU`, `GPU ready in <t>: <adapter>` — so a stall
  names the step that never returned and how long the GPU took.
- NativeRenderer::adapter_name(), for that line and for logs.
- vieww-hal pick_physical_device: under the same VIEWW_TRACE_FRAMES,
  `vulkan: asking <device> (<type>)` before each device's surface
  query, so a stall inside GPU selection names the device it waited
  on. Env-gated; nothing printed otherwise.

Working theory for the next failing trace to settle: on the hybrid
laptop (Intel HD 620 + NVIDIA 920MX), the discrete GPU is runtime-
suspended after the gate's long unattended stretches and vieww's
device selection queries it first; manual back-to-back runs find it
awake. A trace ending at `vulkan: asking nvidia …` would confirm it.

vieww-platform-winit 145/145 (wait_loop 5/5 twice under Xvfb);
vieww-hal 45/46 with only the known PENDING §2.2 blur edge; clippy,
fmt, docs clean.

